## Looping with while let

`while let` repeats a block as long as a pattern matches. It's commonly used with functions that return `Option` to process sequences until exhaustion.

### Basic while let

```xiom
var items: Option[Int] = Some(10);

while let Some(value) = items {
  io.println("Processing: " + value);
  // update items for next iteration
  if value > 0 {
    items = Some(value - 1);
  } else {
    items = None;
  }
}
```

This prints: Processing 10, Processing 9, ..., Processing 0

### while let with Iterators

```xiom
// Process elements one at a time
fn next_item() -> Option[Int] {
  // returns items from a queue
  return None;
}

while let Some(item) = next_item() {
  process(item);
}
```

### while let vs for

`for` iterates over collections; `while let` works with functions returning `Option`:

```xiom
// for loop: iterate a known collection
for item in vec {
  io.println(item);
}

// while let: process until None
while let Some(item) = pop_next() {
  io.println(item);
}
```

### Practical Example: Stack Processing

```xiom
var stack: Option[Int] = Some(3);

while let Some(n) = stack {
  io.println("Popped: " + n);
  if n > 1 {
    stack = Some(n - 1);
  } else {
    stack = None;
  }
}
```

### Infinite Loop Prevention

Ensure the condition eventually becomes `None` — otherwise you get an infinite loop.

```xiom
// DANGER: infinite loop!
// while let Some(x) = always_some() { ... }

// SAFE: progresses toward None
while let Some(x) = pop_from_queue() { ... }
```

